    feat(harness): ship §5-contract adapters for deepseek and pi-agent. Addresses CF-372.
    
    The conformance matrix lists seven harness rows but only four adapters existed in
    scripts/harness/, so `tests/harness-conformance.sh --vars-only` failed two rows with "no
    adapter at ... — no installer to hold to the §5 contract". CF-229 built the contract;
    these two harnesses had nothing to hold to it.
    
    Both new adapters are the install-opencode.sh shape: they source scripts/lib.sh and
    scripts/harness/harness-vars.sh (the single implementation of §5) instead of re-implementing
    any validation, and they run cfv_load_env / cfv_parse_args / cfv_validate / cfv_refused.
    `--contract` validates the declared set and exits 0 (exit 2 with named refusals), so the
    vars leg grades the contract without installing anything.
    
      scripts/harness/install-deepseek.sh — binary `deepseek`. `--check` resolves it with `have`
      and prints a named `FAIL binary — absent` row when it is not there (never a PASS), reading
      a version only when a binary was found. No non-interactive surface is documented for this
      row in either spec, so there is nothing to probe; the adapter says so rather than inventing
      one. `--apply` is REFUSED by name: this repo records no pin, no artifact digest and no
      measured install ladder for deepseek (the matrix names DeepSeek's own harness at npm
      @deepseek-ai/dsh, CLI dsh, absent from both trees). It prints DEEPSEEK: FAIL (n) and exits 1.
    
      scripts/harness/install-pi-agent.sh — binary `pi`. `--check` reports the binary and its
      measured version when present, labels it host-provided (PATH is not an install this repo can
      reproduce), and reports ~/.pi/agent/auth.json as what it is — a credential file, not a
      binary. `--apply` is REFUSED by name for the same reason: no measured pi install ladder here,
      so there is nothing to apply that could be reproduced.
    
    Neither adapter pretends to install. Their check/apply/smoke legs staying red on a host with
    no deepseek binary is the honest documented state; only the contract leg is claimed.
    
    tests/harness/vars-contract-test.sh: INSTALLERS gains both files, so the per-installer §5
    battery covers six adapters instead of four (each one graded on --help coverage, credential
    VALUE refusal, argv credential refusal, ambient-key refusal, and flag->variable recording).
    
    specs/HARNESS.md: the per-adapter inventory table gains the deepseek row and names the
    pi-agent adapter; the following paragraph now states exactly which adapters ship a documented
    --check/--contract (opencode, claude-code, deepseek, pi-agent) and which ship no adapter file
    (aider, goose), instead of the vague "the others". docs/HARNESS-CONFORMANCE.md is machine-written
    by the battery and was not hand-edited.
    
    Acceptance, measured on this host:
      bash -n scripts/harness/install-deepseek.sh && bash -n scripts/harness/install-pi-agent.sh   -> 0
      bash tests/harness/vars-contract-test.sh     -> vars-contract: 32 pass, 0 fail, 1 skip; VARS CONTRACT: PASS
      bash tests/harness-conformance.sh --vars-only -> summary: 8 pass, 0 fail, 0 skip; HARNESS CONFORMANCE: PASS
        (deepseek:vars and pi-agent:vars both PASS, §5 set accepted in --contract, 12 declared)
    
    Note on the brief's premise: `pi` is in fact present on this host's PATH
    (/home/kara/.local/bin/pi, --version 1.5.0), which a bare `command -v` disproves. The adapter
    reports that measured host state; the `--apply` refusal is argued from the absent install
    ladder (no pin, no digest), not from the binary's absence, and no support claim is made from
    a PATH binary.
    
